{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MXJRDLrFLZd3Z3NmP7UUJU","type":"tool_result","content":"214\t### 4. Memory Graph\n215\t\n216\tMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n217\t\n218\tPrimary memory classes:\n219\t\n220\t- `Observation`\n221\t- `Decision`\n222\t- `Constraint`\n223\t- `Pattern`\n224\t- `AntiPattern`\n225\t- `WorkflowOutcome`\n226\t- `FailurePattern`\n227\t- `Procedure`\n228\t- `Preference`\n229\t- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n230\t- `DocsContract`\n231\t- `OpenQuestion`\n232\t- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n233\t\n234\tEach memory record requires:\n235\t\n236\t- stable id\n237\t- content\n238\t- memory class\n239\t- assertion type\n240\t- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n241\t- verification status\n242\t- confidence and confidence reason\n243\t- freshness policy\n244\t- validity conditions\n245\t- invalidation triggers\n246\t- provenance events\n247\t- evidence references\n248\t- linked files\n249\t- linked symbols\n250\t- linked docs\n251\t- linked tests\n252\t- linked memories\n253\t- contradiction links\n254\t- supersession links\n255\t- access history\n256\t- usefulness scores\n257\t- last verified state\n258\t\n259\tMemory links should be first-class records:\n260\t\n261\t- source memory\n262\t- target memory or graph node\n263\t- link type\n264\t- strength\n265\t- reason\n266\t- evidence event\n267\t- created by\n268\t- created at\n269\t- verification status\n270\t\n271\tRequired link types:\n272\t\n273\t- `supports`\n274\t- `contradicts`\n275\t- `supersedes`\n276\t- `refines`\n277\t- `generalizes`\n278\t- `specializes`\n279\t- `co_occurs_with`\n280\t- `derived_from`\n281\t- `applies_to`\n282\t- `validated_by`\n283\t- `invalidated_by`\n284\t\n285\t### 5. Working Memory\n286\t\n287\tWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n288\t\n289\tWorking memory contains:\n290\t\n291\t- task statement\n292\t- interpreted intent\n293\t- active files and symbols\n294\t- active hypotheses\n295\t- active failures\n296\t- current plan\n297\t- selected memories\n298\t- excluded memories and reasons\n299\t- budget decisions\n300\t- unresolved questions\n301\t- verification status\n302\t\n303\tRequired operations:\n304\t\n305\t- `retrieve`\n306\t- `summarize`\n307\t- `filter`\n308\t- `pin`\n309\t- `evict`\n310\t- `expand`\n311\t- `compress`\n312\t- `checkpoint`\n313\t\n314\tWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n315\t\n316\t### 6. Consolidation Engine\n317\t\n318\tThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n319\t\n320\tConsolidation jobs:\n321\t\n322\t- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n323\t- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n324\t- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n325\t- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n326\t- detect duplicate memories\n327\t- detect contradictions\n328\t- detect supersession candidates\n329\t- demote unused or low-value memories\n330\t- mark stale memories after graph changes\n331\t- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n332\t- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n333\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6c80d671-1437-429b-9e3a-231a1f740b4b","uuid":"f06d8bee-b0a4-4e3b-8e1b-25acdbc08c28","timestamp":"2026-05-17T07:18:49.002Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md","content":"### 4. Memory Graph\n\nMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n\nPrimary memory classes:\n\n- `Observation`\n- `Decision`\n- `Constraint`\n- `Pattern`\n- `AntiPattern`\n- `WorkflowOutcome`\n- `FailurePattern`\n- `Procedure`\n- `Preference`\n- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n- `DocsContract`\n- `OpenQuestion`\n- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n\nEach memory record requires:\n\n- stable id\n- content\n- memory class\n- assertion type\n- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n- verification status\n- confidence and confidence reason\n- freshness policy\n- validity conditions\n- invalidation triggers\n- provenance events\n- evidence references\n- linked files\n- linked symbols\n- linked docs\n- linked tests\n- linked memories\n- contradiction links\n- supersession links\n- access history\n- usefulness scores\n- last verified state\n\nMemory links should be first-class records:\n\n- source memory\n- target memory or graph node\n- link type\n- strength\n- reason\n- evidence event\n- created by\n- created at\n- verification status\n\nRequired link types:\n\n- `supports`\n- `contradicts`\n- `supersedes`\n- `refines`\n- `generalizes`\n- `specializes`\n- `co_occurs_with`\n- `derived_from`\n- `applies_to`\n- `validated_by`\n- `invalidated_by`\n\n### 5. Working Memory\n\nWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n\nWorking memory contains:\n\n- task statement\n- interpreted intent\n- active files and symbols\n- active hypotheses\n- active failures\n- current plan\n- selected memories\n- excluded memories and reasons\n- budget decisions\n- unresolved questions\n- verification status\n\nRequired operations:\n\n- `retrieve`\n- `summarize`\n- `filter`\n- `pin`\n- `evict`\n- `expand`\n- `compress`\n- `checkpoint`\n\nWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n\n### 6. Consolidation Engine\n\nThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n\nConsolidation jobs:\n\n- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n- detect duplicate memories\n- detect contradictions\n- detect supersession candidates\n- demote unused or low-value memories\n- mark stale memories after graph changes\n- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n","numLines":120,"startLine":214,"totalLines":969}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01VqXwfug8zJQeh2a51PR7Rk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016xhmgj2aTgHQt9wqWvTAn6","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md","offset":496,"limit":160},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1595,"cache_read_input_tokens":75092,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1595},"output_tokens":65,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6c80d671-1437-429b-9e3a-231a1f740b4b","uuid":"c472bb63-f09a-4ceb-8e41-c4cd6eaecf9a","request_id":"req_011Cb7jXWjAKWQcSe1m3N9FE"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016xhmgj2aTgHQt9wqWvTAn6","type":"tool_result","content":"496\t## Storage Design\n497\t\n498\tUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n499\t\n500\tCore tables:\n501\t\n502\t- `workspaces`\n503\t- `files`\n504\t- `symbols`\n505\t- `documents`\n506\t- `sections`\n507\t- `tests`\n508\t- `graph_edges`\n509\t- `events`\n510\t- `event_payloads`\n511\t- `memories`\n512\t- `memory_links`\n513\t- `memory_evidence`\n514\t- `memory_accesses`\n515\t- `memory_scores`\n516\t- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n517\t- `context_handles`\n518\t- `consolidation_jobs`\n519\t- `verification_jobs`\n520\t\n521\tIndexes:\n522\t\n523\t- graph identity indexes\n524\t- file path indexes\n525\t- symbol name/path indexes\n526\t- doc section indexes\n527\t- event task/session indexes\n528\t- memory scope/type/status indexes\n529\t- memory link source/target indexes\n530\t- freshness and verification indexes\n531\t- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n532\t- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n533\t\n534\tThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n535\t\n536\tEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n537\t\n538\t## Fork Strategy\n539\t\n540\tThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n541\t\n542\tCarry forward:\n543\t\n544\t- Rust daemon structure\n545\t- MCP handling experience\n546\t- workspace boundary protections\n547\t- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n548\t- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n549\t- context handles\n550\t- compact/full render modes\n551\t- structured memory groundwork\n552\t- test discipline around daemon behavior\n553\t- VS Code extension integration pattern\n554\t\n555\tReplace or redesign:\n556\t\n557\t- memory retrieval as keyword search\n558\t- memory as mostly isolated rows\n559\t- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n560\t- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n561\t- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n562\t- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n563\t\n564\t## Implementation Plan\n565\t\n566\t### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n567\t\n568\tDeliverables:\n569\t\n570\t- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n571\t- architecture docs copied and revised\n572\t- crate/module boundary plan\n573\t- storage migration policy\n574\t- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n575\t- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n576\t\n577\tFork decision gate:\n578\t\n579\tPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n580\t\n581\t- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n582\t- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n583\t- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n584\t- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n585\t\n586\tIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n587\t\n588\tVerification:\n589\t\n590\t- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n591\t- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n592\t\n593\t### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n594\t\n595\tIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n596\t\n597\tDeliverables:\n598\t\n599\t- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n600\t- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n601\t- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n602\t- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n603\t- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n604\t\n605\tDefinition of done:\n606\t\n607\t- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n608\t- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n609\t- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n610\t\n611\t### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n612\t\n613\tDeliverables:\n614\t\n615\t- event model\n616\t- append-only SQLite event tables\n617\t- event writer API\n618\t- event reader/query API\n619\t- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n620\t- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n621\t- workflow task/session correlation\n622\t- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n623\t\n624\tDefinition of done:\n625\t\n626\t- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n627\t- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n628\t- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n629\t- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n630\t\n631\t### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n632\t\n633\tDeliverables:\n634\t\n635\t- redesigned memory schema\n636\t- first-class memory links\n637\t- first-class memory evidence\n638\t- memory access/use history\n639\t- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n640\t- memory stream/type taxonomy\n641\t- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n642\t- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n643\t\n644\tDefinition of done:\n645\t\n646\t- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n647\t- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n648\t\n649\t### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n650\t\n651\tDeliverables:\n652\t\n653\t- task intent classifier\n654\t- anchor extractor and resolver\n655\t- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6c80d671-1437-429b-9e3a-231a1f740b4b","uuid":"f61a20a0-a316-4fc5-8f41-10b2e2d81fb9","timestamp":"2026-05-17T07:18:51.868Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md","content":"## Storage Design\n\nUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n\nCore tables:\n\n- `workspaces`\n- `files`\n- `symbols`\n- `documents`\n- `sections`\n- `tests`\n- `graph_edges`\n- `events`\n- `event_payloads`\n- `memories`\n- `memory_links`\n- `memory_evidence`\n- `memory_accesses`\n- `memory_scores`\n- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n- `context_handles`\n- `consolidation_jobs`\n- `verification_jobs`\n\nIndexes:\n\n- graph identity indexes\n- file path indexes\n- symbol name/path indexes\n- doc section indexes\n- event task/session indexes\n- memory scope/type/status indexes\n- memory link source/target indexes\n- freshness and verification indexes\n- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n\nThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n\nEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n\n## Fork Strategy\n\nThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n\nCarry forward:\n\n- Rust daemon structure\n- MCP handling experience\n- workspace boundary protections\n- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n- context handles\n- compact/full render modes\n- structured memory groundwork\n- test discipline around daemon behavior\n- VS Code extension integration pattern\n\nReplace or redesign:\n\n- memory retrieval as keyword search\n- memory as mostly isolated rows\n- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n- architecture docs copied and revised\n- crate/module boundary plan\n- storage migration policy\n- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n\nFork decision gate:\n\nPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n\n- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n\nIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n\nVerification:\n\n- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n\n### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n\nIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n\n### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- event model\n- append-only SQLite event tables\n- event writer API\n- event reader/query API\n- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n- workflow task/session correlation\n- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n\n### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned memory schema\n- first-class memory links\n- first-class memory evidence\n- memory access/use history\n- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n- memory stream/type taxonomy\n- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n\n### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- task intent classifier\n- anchor extractor and resolver\n- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events","numLines":160,"startLine":496,"totalLines":969}}}
