{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BnAoymNyHDWNzLYMNFQTCf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Memory Graph Schema\n2\t\n3\t## Classes\n4\t\n5\tThis document supersedes the model description in\n6\t[2026-04-11-structured-memory.md](2026-04-11-structured-memory.md) for new\n7\tcognitive-workspace memory records. The April design remains the legacy\n8\tsingle-table memory store until the T22 migration moves data into this schema.\n9\t\n10\tThe authoritative memory classes are:\n11\t\n12\t- `Observation`\n13\t- `Decision`\n14\t- `Constraint`\n15\t- `Pattern`\n16\t- `AntiPattern`\n17\t- `WorkflowOutcome`\n18\t- `FailurePattern`\n19\t- `Procedure`\n20\t- `Preference`\n21\t- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n22\t- `DocsContract`\n23\t- `OpenQuestion`\n24\t- `CounterMemory`\n25\t\n26\t`CounterMemory` is a first-class memory class. It is not just a\n27\t`contradicts` edge. Use it when the counter-claim needs its own content,\n28\tauthor, evidence, scope, verification status, and lifecycle. A\n29\t`CounterMemory` can itself be verified, invalidated, contradicted, or\n30\tsuperseded.\n31\t\n32\t## Required Fields\n33\t\n34\tEach memory record requires stable id, content, memory class, assertion type,\n35\tscope, verification status, confidence and confidence reason, freshness policy,\n36\tvalidity conditions, invalidation triggers, provenance events, evidence\n37\treferences, linked files, linked symbols, linked docs, linked tests, linked\n38\tmemories, contradiction links, supersession links, access history, usefulness\n39\tscores, and last verified state.\n40\t\n41\tThe Rust source of truth is\n42\t[classes.rs](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/classes.rs).\n43\tThe SQLite DDL source of truth is\n44\t[schema.sql](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/schema.sql).\n45\t\n46\t## Scopes\n47\t\n48\t`MemoryScope` has five values:\n49\t\n50\t- `session`\n51\t- `branch`\n52\t- `repo`\n53\t- `user`\n54\t- `organization`\n55\t\n56\tThe `memories` table denormalizes the scope target for index-friendly lookup:\n57\t`scope_session_id`, `scope_branch`, `scope_workspace_id`, `scope_user_id`, and\n58\t`scope_org_id`. A row must set exactly the target columns required by its\n59\t`scope` value. For example, `branch` rows set `scope_branch` and\n60\t`scope_workspace_id`; `repo` rows set only `scope_workspace_id`.\n61\t\n62\tScope enforcement is deny-by-default in retrieval and belongs to T21. This\n63\tschema makes the scope target explicit so T21 can enforce it without parsing\n64\tfreeform content.\n65\t\n66\t## Streams\n67\t\n68\tThe memory graph is partitioned into seven typed streams:\n69\t\n70\t- `code_topology`\n71\t- `workflow_episodes`\n72\t- `failure_patterns`\n73\t- `semantic_repo_claims`\n74\t- `architecture_decisions`\n75\t- `user_team_preferences`\n76\t- `docs_and_contract_state`\n77\t\n78\tThe Rust source of truth is\n79\t[streams.rs](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/streams.rs).\n80\t`classify_stream(class, assertion_type)` is deterministic and uses the memory\n81\tclass as the primary signal:\n82\t\n83\t- `WorkflowOutcome`, `Procedure`, and `OpenQuestion` map to\n84\t  `workflow_episodes`.\n85\t- `FailurePattern` maps to `failure_patterns`.\n86\t- `Decision` and `ArchitectureInvariant` map to\n87\t  `architecture_decisions`.\n88\t- `Preference` maps to `user_team_preferences`.\n89\t- `DocsContract` maps to `docs_and_contract_state`.\n90\t- `CounterMemory` normally inherits the stream of the memory it counters. If\n91\t  the target stream is not available at classification time, the fallback is\n92\t  `semantic_repo_claims` and the runtime emits a warning.\n93\t- `Observation`, `Constraint`, `Pattern`, and `AntiPattern` are the ambiguous\n94\t  claim/topology classes. `observation` assertions map them to\n95\t  `code_topology`; `constraint`, `hypothesis`, `decision`, `counter`, and\n96\t  `preference` assertions map them to `semantic_repo_claims`; workflow-shaped\n97\t  assertions (`procedure`, `outcome`, `question`) map them to\n98\t  `workflow_episodes`.\n99\t\n100\tEach stream has a default policy surface for retrieval:\n101\t\n102\t| Stream | Default scope | Freshness default | Consolidation window | Ranking profile |\n103\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n104\t| `code_topology` | `branch` | `branch_scoped`, TTL 6d, recheck 12h | 7d | `code_topology_v1` |\n105\t| `workflow_episodes` | `session` | `session_scoped`, TTL 1d, recheck 4h | 3d | `workflow_episodes_v1` |\n106\t| `failure_patterns` | `repo` | `event_triggered`, TTL 30d, recheck 7d | 14d | `failure_patterns_v1` |\n107\t| `semantic_repo_claims` | `repo` | `repo_scoped`, TTL 14d, recheck 7d | 21d | `semantic_repo_claims_v1` |\n108\t| `architecture_decisions` | `repo` | `repo_scoped`, TTL 90d, recheck 30d | 90d | `architecture_decisions_v1` |\n109\t| `user_team_preferences` | `user` | `time_bound`, TTL 30d, recheck 14d | 30d | `user_team_preferences_v1` |\n110\t| `docs_and_contract_state` | `repo` | `repo_scoped`, TTL 14d, recheck 7d | 14d | `docs_and_contract_state_v1` |\n111\t\n112\tThe ranking profile is a string identifier only. Retrieval logic resolves it in\n113\tPhase 4; stream metadata must not embed ranking algorithms directly.\n114\t\n115\t## Scope Enforcement\n116\t\n117\tThe Rust source of truth is\n118\t[scope.rs](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/scope.rs).\n119\t\n120\tScope queries are deny-by-default:\n121\t\n122\t- A query without an explicit `ScopeFilter` is underspecified and must not\n123\t  return cross-scope data.\n124\t- `ScopeFilter::to_sql_predicate()` targets the denormalized scope columns\n125\t  (`scope_session_id`, `scope_branch`, `scope_workspace_id`, `scope_user_id`,\n126\t  `scope_org_id`) and binds every identifier as a parameter. Formatting\n127\t  user-supplied scope ids into SQL is forbidden.\n128\t- A `ScopeFilter` can include multiple explicit scopes, for example branch plus\n129\t  organization, but organization visibility is opt-in. A branch filter alone\n130\t  must not surface organization-scoped rows.\n131\t- `ScopeFilter::enforce_subset()` prevents widening. If the session is allowed\n132\t  to read only branch-scoped memories for one workspace/branch, adding repo,\n133\t  organization, another branch, or another user is a contract violation.\n134\t- Internal unscoped query paths must be named explicitly as admin or migration\n135\t  paths. Implicit unscoped reads are not allowed.\n136\t\n137\t## Verification Statuses\n138\t\n139\t`VerificationStatus` has eight values:\n140\t\n141\t- `unverified`\n142\t- `in_review`\n143\t- `verified`\n144\t- `stale`\n145\t- `contradicted`\n146\t- `superseded`\n147\t- `expired`\n148\t- `invalidated`\n149\t\n150\tThe database constrains the column to these wire names. Retrieval and\n151\tverification code must treat these as lifecycle states, not display labels.\n152\t\n153\t## Links\n154\t\n155\t`memory_links` is the normalized edge table for contradiction, supersession,\n156\t support, and related graph semantics. Each row stores:\n157\t\n158\t- stable `link_id`\n159\t- `source_memory_id`\n160\t- `target_kind` plus `target_id`\n161\t- `link_type`\n162\t- bounded `strength` in `[0, 1]`\n163\t- human-readable `reason`\n164\t- optional `evidence_event_id`\n165\t- `created_by_kind` plus `created_by_detail`\n166\t- `created_at`\n167\t- `verification_status`\n168\t\n169\tThe Rust source of truth is\n170\t[links.rs](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/links.rs).\n171\tLinks replace the denormalized contradiction and supersession JSON blobs as the\n172\tauthoritative query surface for graph traversal and explanation.\n173\t\n174\t## Evidence\n175\t\n176\t`memory_evidence` stores verification anchors as first-class rows instead of\n177\tembedding all provenance into one memory JSON column. Each row stores stable\n178\t`evidence_id`, owning `memory_id`, optional `event_id`, `anchor_kind`,\n179\t`anchor_json`, `captured_at`, and `captured_by_*`.\n180\t\n181\t`EvidenceAnchor` is a typed enum with these variants:\n182\t\n183\t- `FileSpan { file, byte_start, byte_end, sha256 }`\n184\t- `SymbolRef(symbol_id)`\n185\t- `DocSection { id, sha256 }`\n186\t- `TestResult { test, passed, run_event }`\n187\t- `EventReference(event_id)`\n188\t\n189\tThe span and doc-section hashes are required for exact re-verification in Phase\n190\t7. The Rust source of truth is\n191\t[evidence.rs](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/evidence.rs).\n192\t\n193\t## Accesses\n194\t\n195\t`memory_accesses` records retrieval-time inclusion history. Each row stores a\n196\tstable `access_id`, owning `memory_id`, `accessed_at`, `accessed_in_event`,\n197\t`accessor`, `inclusion_reason`, nullable `was_used`, and nullable\n198\t`downstream_outcome_event`.\n199\t\n200\t`was_used` remains nullable on first write because retrieval and downstream"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6c80d671-1437-429b-9e3a-231a1f740b4b","uuid":"81dc895f-968a-4eeb-9792-7549106ddcf7","timestamp":"2026-05-17T07:20:47.358Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/architecture/2026-05-16-memory-graph-schema.md","content":"# Memory Graph Schema\n\n## Classes\n\nThis document supersedes the model description in\n[2026-04-11-structured-memory.md](2026-04-11-structured-memory.md) for new\ncognitive-workspace memory records. The April design remains the legacy\nsingle-table memory store until the T22 migration moves data into this schema.\n\nThe authoritative memory classes are:\n\n- `Observation`\n- `Decision`\n- `Constraint`\n- `Pattern`\n- `AntiPattern`\n- `WorkflowOutcome`\n- `FailurePattern`\n- `Procedure`\n- `Preference`\n- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n- `DocsContract`\n- `OpenQuestion`\n- `CounterMemory`\n\n`CounterMemory` is a first-class memory class. It is not just a\n`contradicts` edge. Use it when the counter-claim needs its own content,\nauthor, evidence, scope, verification status, and lifecycle. A\n`CounterMemory` can itself be verified, invalidated, contradicted, or\nsuperseded.\n\n## Required Fields\n\nEach memory record requires stable id, content, memory class, assertion type,\nscope, verification status, confidence and confidence reason, freshness policy,\nvalidity conditions, invalidation triggers, provenance events, evidence\nreferences, linked files, linked symbols, linked docs, linked tests, linked\nmemories, contradiction links, supersession links, access history, usefulness\nscores, and last verified state.\n\nThe Rust source of truth is\n[classes.rs](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/classes.rs).\nThe SQLite DDL source of truth is\n[schema.sql](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/schema.sql).\n\n## Scopes\n\n`MemoryScope` has five values:\n\n- `session`\n- `branch`\n- `repo`\n- `user`\n- `organization`\n\nThe `memories` table denormalizes the scope target for index-friendly lookup:\n`scope_session_id`, `scope_branch`, `scope_workspace_id`, `scope_user_id`, and\n`scope_org_id`. A row must set exactly the target columns required by its\n`scope` value. For example, `branch` rows set `scope_branch` and\n`scope_workspace_id`; `repo` rows set only `scope_workspace_id`.\n\nScope enforcement is deny-by-default in retrieval and belongs to T21. This\nschema makes the scope target explicit so T21 can enforce it without parsing\nfreeform content.\n\n## Streams\n\nThe memory graph is partitioned into seven typed streams:\n\n- `code_topology`\n- `workflow_episodes`\n- `failure_patterns`\n- `semantic_repo_claims`\n- `architecture_decisions`\n- `user_team_preferences`\n- `docs_and_contract_state`\n\nThe Rust source of truth is\n[streams.rs](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/streams.rs).\n`classify_stream(class, assertion_type)` is deterministic and uses the memory\nclass as the primary signal:\n\n- `WorkflowOutcome`, `Procedure`, and `OpenQuestion` map to\n  `workflow_episodes`.\n- `FailurePattern` maps to `failure_patterns`.\n- `Decision` and `ArchitectureInvariant` map to\n  `architecture_decisions`.\n- `Preference` maps to `user_team_preferences`.\n- `DocsContract` maps to `docs_and_contract_state`.\n- `CounterMemory` normally inherits the stream of the memory it counters. If\n  the target stream is not available at classification time, the fallback is\n  `semantic_repo_claims` and the runtime emits a warning.\n- `Observation`, `Constraint`, `Pattern`, and `AntiPattern` are the ambiguous\n  claim/topology classes. `observation` assertions map them to\n  `code_topology`; `constraint`, `hypothesis`, `decision`, `counter`, and\n  `preference` assertions map them to `semantic_repo_claims`; workflow-shaped\n  assertions (`procedure`, `outcome`, `question`) map them to\n  `workflow_episodes`.\n\nEach stream has a default policy surface for retrieval:\n\n| Stream | Default scope | Freshness default | Consolidation window | Ranking profile |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `code_topology` | `branch` | `branch_scoped`, TTL 6d, recheck 12h | 7d | `code_topology_v1` |\n| `workflow_episodes` | `session` | `session_scoped`, TTL 1d, recheck 4h | 3d | `workflow_episodes_v1` |\n| `failure_patterns` | `repo` | `event_triggered`, TTL 30d, recheck 7d | 14d | `failure_patterns_v1` |\n| `semantic_repo_claims` | `repo` | `repo_scoped`, TTL 14d, recheck 7d | 21d | `semantic_repo_claims_v1` |\n| `architecture_decisions` | `repo` | `repo_scoped`, TTL 90d, recheck 30d | 90d | `architecture_decisions_v1` |\n| `user_team_preferences` | `user` | `time_bound`, TTL 30d, recheck 14d | 30d | `user_team_preferences_v1` |\n| `docs_and_contract_state` | `repo` | `repo_scoped`, TTL 14d, recheck 7d | 14d | `docs_and_contract_state_v1` |\n\nThe ranking profile is a string identifier only. Retrieval logic resolves it in\nPhase 4; stream metadata must not embed ranking algorithms directly.\n\n## Scope Enforcement\n\nThe Rust source of truth is\n[scope.rs](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/scope.rs).\n\nScope queries are deny-by-default:\n\n- A query without an explicit `ScopeFilter` is underspecified and must not\n  return cross-scope data.\n- `ScopeFilter::to_sql_predicate()` targets the denormalized scope columns\n  (`scope_session_id`, `scope_branch`, `scope_workspace_id`, `scope_user_id`,\n  `scope_org_id`) and binds every identifier as a parameter. Formatting\n  user-supplied scope ids into SQL is forbidden.\n- A `ScopeFilter` can include multiple explicit scopes, for example branch plus\n  organization, but organization visibility is opt-in. A branch filter alone\n  must not surface organization-scoped rows.\n- `ScopeFilter::enforce_subset()` prevents widening. If the session is allowed\n  to read only branch-scoped memories for one workspace/branch, adding repo,\n  organization, another branch, or another user is a contract violation.\n- Internal unscoped query paths must be named explicitly as admin or migration\n  paths. Implicit unscoped reads are not allowed.\n\n## Verification Statuses\n\n`VerificationStatus` has eight values:\n\n- `unverified`\n- `in_review`\n- `verified`\n- `stale`\n- `contradicted`\n- `superseded`\n- `expired`\n- `invalidated`\n\nThe database constrains the column to these wire names. Retrieval and\nverification code must treat these as lifecycle states, not display labels.\n\n## Links\n\n`memory_links` is the normalized edge table for contradiction, supersession,\n support, and related graph semantics. Each row stores:\n\n- stable `link_id`\n- `source_memory_id`\n- `target_kind` plus `target_id`\n- `link_type`\n- bounded `strength` in `[0, 1]`\n- human-readable `reason`\n- optional `evidence_event_id`\n- `created_by_kind` plus `created_by_detail`\n- `created_at`\n- `verification_status`\n\nThe Rust source of truth is\n[links.rs](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/links.rs).\nLinks replace the denormalized contradiction and supersession JSON blobs as the\nauthoritative query surface for graph traversal and explanation.\n\n## Evidence\n\n`memory_evidence` stores verification anchors as first-class rows instead of\nembedding all provenance into one memory JSON column. Each row stores stable\n`evidence_id`, owning `memory_id`, optional `event_id`, `anchor_kind`,\n`anchor_json`, `captured_at`, and `captured_by_*`.\n\n`EvidenceAnchor` is a typed enum with these variants:\n\n- `FileSpan { file, byte_start, byte_end, sha256 }`\n- `SymbolRef(symbol_id)`\n- `DocSection { id, sha256 }`\n- `TestResult { test, passed, run_event }`\n- `EventReference(event_id)`\n\nThe span and doc-section hashes are required for exact re-verification in Phase\n7. The Rust source of truth is\n[evidence.rs](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/evidence.rs).\n\n## Accesses\n\n`memory_accesses` records retrieval-time inclusion history. Each row stores a\nstable `access_id`, owning `memory_id`, `accessed_at`, `accessed_in_event`,\n`accessor`, `inclusion_reason`, nullable `was_used`, and nullable\n`downstream_outcome_event`.\n\n`was_used` remains nullable on first write because retrieval and downstream","numLines":200,"startLine":1,"totalLines":371}}}
