{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VPy3XfGGwJKo7VXeWt8y8G","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t`was_used` remains nullable on first write because retrieval and downstream\n201\toutcome correlation are separate phases. The Rust source of truth is\n202\t[accesses.rs](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/accesses.rs).\n203\t\n204\t## Scores\n205\t\n206\t`memory_scores` stores score history instead of a single current value. The\n207\tprimary key is `(memory_id, score_kind, computed_at)`, which allows multiple\n208\trows per memory and kind while preserving deterministic latest-value lookup.\n209\t\n210\t`ScoreKind` currently includes:\n211\t\n212\t- `usefulness_prior`\n213\t- `recent_usefulness`\n214\t- `retrieval_accuracy`\n215\t- `regression_risk`\n216\t\n217\tEach row stores `value`, `computed_at`, `computed_from_window_secs`, and\n218\t`sample_size`. The Rust source of truth is\n219\t[scores.rs](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/scores.rs).\n220\t\n221\t## Freshness, Validity, Invalidation\n222\t\n223\tFreshness policy is stored in `freshness_policy_json` and decoded into\n224\t`FreshnessPolicy` with `FreshnessKind` values `session_scoped`,\n225\t`branch_scoped`, `repo_scoped`, `time_bound`, and `event_triggered`.\n226\t\n227\tValidity conditions are stored in `validity_conditions_json` and decoded into\n228\tmachine-checkable predicates:\n229\t\n230\t- file exists\n231\t- symbol exists\n232\t- doc heading exists\n233\t- test passes\n234\t- time before\n235\t\n236\tInvalidation triggers are stored in `invalidation_triggers_json`. Trigger kinds\n237\tare file changed, symbol changed, doc section changed, test failed, and time\n238\texpired. Each trigger carries a `StableRef` target so Phase 7 verification can\n239\tsubscribe deterministically.\n240\t\n241\tAll `*_json` columns are untrusted storage. Callers must decode them through\n242\ttyped Rust structs and return typed errors on decode failure.\n243\t\n244\t## CounterMemory Semantics\n245\t\n246\t`CounterMemory` records are authored claims that a previous memory is wrong or\n247\tno longer applicable. They carry their own evidence and validity conditions.\n248\t\n249\tA contradiction link connects two memories. A `CounterMemory` is a record. Both\n250\tcan exist at the same time: the record explains the counter-claim, while the\n251\tlink table added by T20 can connect the counter-claim to the memory it\n252\tcontradicts.\n253\t\n254\tRetrieval must not infer that every `CounterMemory` is verified. It starts with\n255\tthe same verification lifecycle as any other class and only outranks the prior\n256\tclaim when verification, scope, confidence, freshness, and link semantics\n257\tsupport that result.\n258\t\n259\t## Lifecycle and transitions\n260\t\n261\t`VerificationStatus` is a state machine, not a freeform tag. The allowed\n262\ttransitions are:\n263\t\n264\t| From | To |\n265\t| --- | --- |\n266\t| `unverified` | `in_review`, `verified` |\n267\t| `in_review` | `verified`, `stale`, `contradicted`, `expired`, `invalidated` |\n268\t| `verified` | `stale`, `contradicted`, `superseded`, `expired`, `invalidated` |\n269\t| `stale` | `verified`, `invalidated` |\n270\t| `contradicted` | none |\n271\t| `superseded` | none |\n272\t| `expired` | none |\n273\t| `invalidated` | none |\n274\t\n275\tAdditional lifecycle rules:\n276\t\n277\t- `superseded` requires `superseded_by` to identify the newer memory.\n278\t- Every lifecycle change appends provenance through a lifecycle event in the\n279\t  event log.\n280\t- Every lifecycle change records evidence that points at the triggering event.\n281\t- `MemoryConsolidated` is reserved for Phase 6 consolidation workflows and is\n282\t  not used for normal verification-status transitions.\n283\t\n284\t## Replay semantics\n285\t\n286\tThe event log is the replay source of truth for memory-graph lifecycle state.\n287\t`MemoryCreated`, `MemoryUpdated`, and `MemoryInvalidated` events carry an exact\n288\tpost-write replay snapshot of the affected memory row plus its normalized\n289\tevidence rows and outgoing links.\n290\t\n291\tReplay applies one event at a time and each event snapshot is transactional:\n292\t\n293\t- upsert the `memories` row from the replay snapshot\n294\t- replace all `memory_evidence` rows for that memory with the snapshotted set\n295\t- replace all outgoing `memory_links` rows for that source memory with the\n296\t  snapshotted set\n297\t\n298\tThis design makes replay idempotent and bounded. A corrupted replay snapshot\n299\tfails that event without leaving a half-applied row/evidence/link state behind.\n300\t\n301\tSnapshot bootstrap must be equivalent to full replay. A midpoint memory snapshot\n302\tfollowed by replay of the tail events must produce the same `memories`,\n303\t`memory_links`, and `memory_evidence` contents as replaying the full event log\n304\tfrom an empty database.\n305\t\n306\t## Schema Parity Table\n307\t\n308\t| SQLite column | Rust field |\n309\t| --- | --- |\n310\t| `memory_id` | `memory_id` |\n311\t| `content` | `content` |\n312\t| `class` | `class` |\n313\t| `assertion_type` | `assertion_type` |\n314\t| `scope` | `scope` |\n315\t| `scope_session_id` | `scope_session_id` |\n316\t| `scope_branch` | `scope_branch` |\n317\t| `scope_workspace_id` | `scope_workspace_id` |\n318\t| `scope_user_id` | `scope_user_id` |\n319\t| `scope_org_id` | `scope_org_id` |\n320\t| `verification_status` | `verification_status` |\n321\t| `confidence` | `confidence` |\n322\t| `confidence_reason` | `confidence_reason` |\n323\t| `freshness_policy_json` | `freshness_policy` |\n324\t| `validity_conditions_json` | `validity_conditions` |\n325\t| `invalidation_triggers_json` | `invalidation_triggers` |\n326\t| `provenance_event_ids_json` | `provenance_event_ids` |\n327\t| `evidence_references_json` | `evidence_references` |\n328\t| `linked_files_json` | `linked_files` |\n329\t| `linked_symbols_json` | `linked_symbols` |\n330\t| `linked_docs_json` | `linked_docs` |\n331\t| `linked_tests_json` | `linked_tests` |\n332\t| `linked_memories_json` | `linked_memories` |\n333\t| `contradiction_links_json` | `contradiction_links` |\n334\t| `supersession_links_json` | `supersession_links` |\n335\t| `access_history_json` | `access_history` |\n336\t| `last_verified_event_id` | `last_verified_event_id` |\n337\t| `last_verified_state` | `last_verified_state` |\n338\t| `usefulness_score` | `usefulness_score` |\n339\t| `usefulness_score_updated_at` | `usefulness_score_updated_at` |\n340\t| `created_at` | `created_at` |\n341\t| `created_by` | `created_by` |\n342\t| `updated_at` | `updated_at` |\n343\t| `updated_by` | `updated_by` |\n344\t| `superseded_by` | `superseded_by` |\n345\t| `schema_version` | `schema_version` |\n346\t\n347\tNormalized companion tables added by T20:\n348\t\n349\t| SQLite table | Rust type |\n350\t| --- | --- |\n351\t| `memory_links` | `MemoryLink` |\n352\t| `memory_evidence` | `MemoryEvidence` |\n353\t| `memory_accesses` | `MemoryAccess` |\n354\t| `memory_scores` | `MemoryScore` |\n355\t\n356\t## Migration Plan Reference\n357\t\n358\tT22 owns migration from the legacy `memory/store.rs` single-table shape into\n359\tthis redesigned memory graph schema. The migration must preserve old memory\n360\tcontent, map legacy memory types into `MemoryClass` and `AssertionType`, seed\n361\tfreshness and verification fields conservatively, and write provenance event\n362\treferences from the Phase 2 event log where available.\n363\t\n364\t## Index Inventory\n365\t\n366\tT23 owns the authoritative index inventory for CRUD and transition paths. This\n367\tschema intentionally includes denormalized scope columns and scalar lifecycle\n368\tcolumns so T23 can add bounded indexes for scope filtering, verification\n369\tstatus, usefulness ordering, supersession lookup, and migration backfills\n370\twithout changing the memory contract.\n371\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6c80d671-1437-429b-9e3a-231a1f740b4b","uuid":"21907096-b345-489e-8ad0-7fd313dfbc9b","timestamp":"2026-05-17T07:20:52.232Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/architecture/2026-05-16-memory-graph-schema.md","content":"`was_used` remains nullable on first write because retrieval and downstream\noutcome correlation are separate phases. The Rust source of truth is\n[accesses.rs](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/accesses.rs).\n\n## Scores\n\n`memory_scores` stores score history instead of a single current value. The\nprimary key is `(memory_id, score_kind, computed_at)`, which allows multiple\nrows per memory and kind while preserving deterministic latest-value lookup.\n\n`ScoreKind` currently includes:\n\n- `usefulness_prior`\n- `recent_usefulness`\n- `retrieval_accuracy`\n- `regression_risk`\n\nEach row stores `value`, `computed_at`, `computed_from_window_secs`, and\n`sample_size`. The Rust source of truth is\n[scores.rs](/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/scores.rs).\n\n## Freshness, Validity, Invalidation\n\nFreshness policy is stored in `freshness_policy_json` and decoded into\n`FreshnessPolicy` with `FreshnessKind` values `session_scoped`,\n`branch_scoped`, `repo_scoped`, `time_bound`, and `event_triggered`.\n\nValidity conditions are stored in `validity_conditions_json` and decoded into\nmachine-checkable predicates:\n\n- file exists\n- symbol exists\n- doc heading exists\n- test passes\n- time before\n\nInvalidation triggers are stored in `invalidation_triggers_json`. Trigger kinds\nare file changed, symbol changed, doc section changed, test failed, and time\nexpired. Each trigger carries a `StableRef` target so Phase 7 verification can\nsubscribe deterministically.\n\nAll `*_json` columns are untrusted storage. Callers must decode them through\ntyped Rust structs and return typed errors on decode failure.\n\n## CounterMemory Semantics\n\n`CounterMemory` records are authored claims that a previous memory is wrong or\nno longer applicable. They carry their own evidence and validity conditions.\n\nA contradiction link connects two memories. A `CounterMemory` is a record. Both\ncan exist at the same time: the record explains the counter-claim, while the\nlink table added by T20 can connect the counter-claim to the memory it\ncontradicts.\n\nRetrieval must not infer that every `CounterMemory` is verified. It starts with\nthe same verification lifecycle as any other class and only outranks the prior\nclaim when verification, scope, confidence, freshness, and link semantics\nsupport that result.\n\n## Lifecycle and transitions\n\n`VerificationStatus` is a state machine, not a freeform tag. The allowed\ntransitions are:\n\n| From | To |\n| --- | --- |\n| `unverified` | `in_review`, `verified` |\n| `in_review` | `verified`, `stale`, `contradicted`, `expired`, `invalidated` |\n| `verified` | `stale`, `contradicted`, `superseded`, `expired`, `invalidated` |\n| `stale` | `verified`, `invalidated` |\n| `contradicted` | none |\n| `superseded` | none |\n| `expired` | none |\n| `invalidated` | none |\n\nAdditional lifecycle rules:\n\n- `superseded` requires `superseded_by` to identify the newer memory.\n- Every lifecycle change appends provenance through a lifecycle event in the\n  event log.\n- Every lifecycle change records evidence that points at the triggering event.\n- `MemoryConsolidated` is reserved for Phase 6 consolidation workflows and is\n  not used for normal verification-status transitions.\n\n## Replay semantics\n\nThe event log is the replay source of truth for memory-graph lifecycle state.\n`MemoryCreated`, `MemoryUpdated`, and `MemoryInvalidated` events carry an exact\npost-write replay snapshot of the affected memory row plus its normalized\nevidence rows and outgoing links.\n\nReplay applies one event at a time and each event snapshot is transactional:\n\n- upsert the `memories` row from the replay snapshot\n- replace all `memory_evidence` rows for that memory with the snapshotted set\n- replace all outgoing `memory_links` rows for that source memory with the\n  snapshotted set\n\nThis design makes replay idempotent and bounded. A corrupted replay snapshot\nfails that event without leaving a half-applied row/evidence/link state behind.\n\nSnapshot bootstrap must be equivalent to full replay. A midpoint memory snapshot\nfollowed by replay of the tail events must produce the same `memories`,\n`memory_links`, and `memory_evidence` contents as replaying the full event log\nfrom an empty database.\n\n## Schema Parity Table\n\n| SQLite column | Rust field |\n| --- | --- |\n| `memory_id` | `memory_id` |\n| `content` | `content` |\n| `class` | `class` |\n| `assertion_type` | `assertion_type` |\n| `scope` | `scope` |\n| `scope_session_id` | `scope_session_id` |\n| `scope_branch` | `scope_branch` |\n| `scope_workspace_id` | `scope_workspace_id` |\n| `scope_user_id` | `scope_user_id` |\n| `scope_org_id` | `scope_org_id` |\n| `verification_status` | `verification_status` |\n| `confidence` | `confidence` |\n| `confidence_reason` | `confidence_reason` |\n| `freshness_policy_json` | `freshness_policy` |\n| `validity_conditions_json` | `validity_conditions` |\n| `invalidation_triggers_json` | `invalidation_triggers` |\n| `provenance_event_ids_json` | `provenance_event_ids` |\n| `evidence_references_json` | `evidence_references` |\n| `linked_files_json` | `linked_files` |\n| `linked_symbols_json` | `linked_symbols` |\n| `linked_docs_json` | `linked_docs` |\n| `linked_tests_json` | `linked_tests` |\n| `linked_memories_json` | `linked_memories` |\n| `contradiction_links_json` | `contradiction_links` |\n| `supersession_links_json` | `supersession_links` |\n| `access_history_json` | `access_history` |\n| `last_verified_event_id` | `last_verified_event_id` |\n| `last_verified_state` | `last_verified_state` |\n| `usefulness_score` | `usefulness_score` |\n| `usefulness_score_updated_at` | `usefulness_score_updated_at` |\n| `created_at` | `created_at` |\n| `created_by` | `created_by` |\n| `updated_at` | `updated_at` |\n| `updated_by` | `updated_by` |\n| `superseded_by` | `superseded_by` |\n| `schema_version` | `schema_version` |\n\nNormalized companion tables added by T20:\n\n| SQLite table | Rust type |\n| --- | --- |\n| `memory_links` | `MemoryLink` |\n| `memory_evidence` | `MemoryEvidence` |\n| `memory_accesses` | `MemoryAccess` |\n| `memory_scores` | `MemoryScore` |\n\n## Migration Plan Reference\n\nT22 owns migration from the legacy `memory/store.rs` single-table shape into\nthis redesigned memory graph schema. The migration must preserve old memory\ncontent, map legacy memory types into `MemoryClass` and `AssertionType`, seed\nfreshness and verification fields conservatively, and write provenance event\nreferences from the Phase 2 event log where available.\n\n## Index Inventory\n\nT23 owns the authoritative index inventory for CRUD and transition paths. This\nschema intentionally includes denormalized scope columns and scalar lifecycle\ncolumns so T23 can add bounded indexes for scope filtering, verification\nstatus, usefulness ordering, supersession lookup, and migration backfills\nwithout changing the memory contract.\n","numLines":172,"startLine":200,"totalLines":371}}}
