{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_017Xz8rnfxiiiqdmriqCjPtD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014HdX8fhXzDh53PqxGrXMYR","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R25-memory-graph.md","content":"# R25 — Phase 3 Memory Graph Storage Review\n\n**Reviewer:** Claude (advanced model class)\n**Date:** 2026-05-17\n**Phase under review:** Phase 3 — Memory Graph Storage (T19, T20, T21, T22, T23, T24)\n**Spec anchor:** `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-3-memory-graph-storage`\n\n## Spec alignment\n\nThe table below maps each Phase 3 deliverable, definition-of-done line, and spec invariant (cognitive-workspace plan `## 4. Memory Graph`, `## Storage Design`, `### ViLoMem`, `### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage`, `### Scope Leakage`) to the artifact that satisfies it.\n\n| Spec line | Artifact | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 13 memory classes incl. CounterMemory (`plan.md ## 4. Memory Graph`) | `memory_graph/classes.rs:31-45` `MemoryClass`, `schema.sql:18-34` CHECK | Satisfied |\n| Required memory fields (stable id … last verified state, `plan.md ## 4. Memory Graph`) | `memory_graph/classes.rs:359-396` `MemoryRecord`, `schema.sql:15-135` columns, `memory-graph-schema.md ## Schema Parity Table` | Satisfied |\n| Five scopes (session, branch, repo, user, organization) | `memory_graph/classes.rs:175-181` `MemoryScope`, `schema.sql:48-134` CHECK with disjoint scope-column rules | Satisfied |\n| Verification statuses (eight, lifecycle) | `memory_graph/classes.rs:217-226` `VerificationStatus`, `schema.sql:56-66` CHECK, `memory_graph/transitions.rs:53-91` transition matrix matches `memory-graph-schema.md ## Lifecycle and transitions` table | Satisfied |\n| First-class memory links (11 link types) | `memory_graph/links.rs:31-58` `MemoryLinkType`, `schema.sql:149-191` `memory_links`, indexes `idx_memory_links_{source,target,type}` | Satisfied |\n| First-class memory evidence | `memory_graph/evidence.rs:27-60` `EvidenceAnchor`, `schema.sql:193-212` `memory_evidence`, indexes `idx_memory_evidence_{memory,event}` | Satisfied |\n| Memory access/use history | `memory_graph/accesses.rs:27-37` `MemoryAccess`, `schema.sql:214-226` `memory_accesses`, indexes `idx_memory_accesses_{memory_time,event}` | Satisfied |\n| Usefulness/recent/retrieval/regression score history | `memory_graph/scores.rs:17-62` `ScoreKind` and `MemoryScore`, `schema.sql:228-243` `memory_scores` (PK includes `computed_at`), index `idx_memory_scores_memory_kind` | Satisfied |\n| Typed memory streams (seven, ViLoMem) | `memory_graph/streams.rs:20-52` `MemoryStream`, `streams.rs:96-120` `classify_stream` total over `MemoryClass` (exhaustive `match`), `memory-graph-schema.md ## Streams` table | Satisfied |\n| Stream defaults (freshness, consolidation, scope, ranking profile) | `memory_graph/streams.rs:122-188` `default_policy`, schema doc table at `memory-graph-schema.md ## Streams` | Satisfied |\n| Deny-by-default scope queries (`plan.md ### Scope Leakage`) | `memory_graph/scope.rs:141-192` `to_sql_predicate` returns `1 = 0` for empty filter; `scope.rs:194-289` `enforce_subset` rejects widening and identity drift; `memory_graph/store.rs:1192-1198` `ensure_scope_filter` rejects underspecified reads at the API boundary; `memory_graph/scope_tests.rs:114-282` proves admin path is explicit, branch/user never leak, organization is opt-in | Satisfied |\n| Migration importer (idempotent, dry-runnable, restartable) | `memory_graph/migration.rs` `MemoryMigrator::plan` / `MemoryMigrator::run`; `migration_progress` table; `migration_mapping.rs::map_row`; `memory-migration-guide.md ## Column mapping` covers every legacy column; `migration_tests.rs:60-184` exercises empty, curated, idempotent second run, conflict rollback, dry-run-parity, and unmappable-row reporting | Satisfied |\n| Contradiction and supersession as graph edges | `memory_graph/links.rs` `Contradicts` / `Supersedes` variants; `contradiction_tests.rs:12-303` and `supersession_tests.rs:15-100` | Satisfied |\n| Memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale, replay tests | `links_tests.rs`, `store_tests.rs`, `contradiction_tests.rs`, `supersession_tests.rs`, `replay_tests.rs:14-163` | Satisfied |\n| DoD: \"memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\" | 13-variant `MemoryClass` and round-tripped `create`/`update`/`delete`/`transition_status` paths in `store_tests.rs:11-312` | Satisfied |\n| DoD: \"every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\" | `evidence_references_json` + `memory_evidence` rows seeded by `collect_initial_evidence`, `event_reference_evidence`, and `write_transition`; lifecycle events carry `replay_snapshot_json`; outgoing-link traversal via `get_links_from`/`get_links_to` | Satisfied |\n| Plan `## Storage Design` — `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores` first-class tables | `schema.sql:149-243` | Satisfied |\n| Plan `## Storage Design` — replay capability | `memory_graph/replay.rs::capture_replay_snapshot` and `replay_events`; `MemoryCreatedPayload`/`MemoryUpdatedPayload`/`MemoryInvalidatedPayload` carry `replay_snapshot_json`; `replay_tests.rs:14-68` proves byte-for-byte equality, snapshot+tail equivalence, and typed `SnapshotDecode` error on corruption | Satisfied |\n| Plan `## Storage Design` — \"memory scope/type/status indexes\", \"freshness and verification indexes\" | **Not yet added to `schema.sql`.** See Findings F-3. Schema doc `## Index Inventory` explicitly hands this to T23, which did not complete it. | Partial |\n\n## Coding-standard alignment\n\nChecked against `/home/pete/cadres/CLAUDE.md` Cadres Coding Standard (Hard limits, Single source of truth, Error handling, Naming, No broken windows, Schema/model parity) and the `## Markdown Heading References` rule in the project CLAUDE.md.\n\n- **File length (Hard limits, 800 lines):** `store.rs` is **1303 lines** — well over the ceiling, with no corresponding split-justification commit message. `migration.rs` at 739, `links.rs` at 476, `migration_mapping.rs` at 448, and `classes.rs` at 448 are all within ceiling. See finding F-1.\n- **Function length (Hard limits, 50 lines):** `memory_params` (51), `update` (51), `decode_memory_row` (47), `record_from_draft` (42), `validate_scope_state` (44), `replace_memory` (43), `workspace_id_for_scope` (42) — `memory_params` and `update` cross the 50-line ceiling. The two over-ceiling functions are coherent enumerations over the 36 memory columns, but `update` mixes idempotency lookup, transaction setup, patch application, evidence stamping, and event preparation and would benefit from extraction. See finding F-2.\n- **Nesting (3 levels max):** All audited functions stay within three meaningful levels (`match` arms count as one). No pyramid-of-doom.\n- **SQL binding (no raw string interpolation of caller data):** `store.rs`, `links.rs`, `evidence.rs`, `accesses.rs`, `scores.rs`, and `migration.rs` use `params!`/`params_from_iter` for every user-controlled value. The remaining `format!`-built SQL is restricted to schema-known table-name interpolation in test helpers (`migration_tests.rs:266`, `replay_tests.rs:298`, `tests_common.rs:208`) — acceptable since the table name is a compile-time literal. **`store.rs::list_filtered` builds `WHERE …` from a static `filter_sql` argument plus a parameterized `ScopeFilter::to_sql_predicate`** — the filter SQL fragments are compile-time constants (`\"class = ?\"`, `\"verification_status = ?\"`, `\"1 = 1\"`), so this remains safe.\n- **Schema/model parity:** Every column in `schema.sql` has a Rust field in `MemoryRecord` (`memory_graph/classes.rs:359-396`); every Rust field has a column or is materialised in a normalized table. The schema doc `## Schema Parity Table` mirrors the mapping. Migrated `MappedMemoryRow` covers every destination column.\n- **Error handling:** `MemoryStoreError`, `MemoryLinkError`, `MemoryEvidenceError`, `MemoryAccessError`, `MemoryScoreError`, `MemoryReplayError`, `MigrationError`, `ScopeError`, `MemoryTransitionError`, and `MemoryGraphParseError` are typed via `thiserror::Error`. No bare `unwrap` on fallible IO paths in production code. The `expect(\"freshness encodes\")` / `\"validity encodes\"` chain in `memory_params` (`store.rs:1012-1032`) is on `serde_json::to_string` of typed Rust structs, which is infallible for these shapes — acceptable. `MemoryStream::FromStr::Err = &'static str` is the one inconsistency; see finding F-4.\n- **Tracing (observability rule):** All write-side helpers emit instrumented spans — `trace_span!(\"memory_graph.insert_link\")` (`links.rs:274`), `trace_span!(\"memory_graph.insert_evidence\")` (`evidence.rs:92`), `trace_span!(\"memory_graph.record_access\")` (`accesses.rs:50`), `trace_span!(\"memory_graph.mark_used\")` (`accesses.rs:168`), `trace_span!(\"memory_graph.write_score\")` (`scores.rs:73`), `info_span!(\"memory_store.transition\")` (`transitions.rs:30`), `info_span!(\"memory_migration\", …)` (`migration.rs:156`). Foreign-key violations emit a `warn_on_fk_violation` log line (`mod.rs:187-193`). Migration skip events emit a `warn!` with `mapping_guide` heading reference.\n- **Naming:** All identifiers in this slice follow snake_case wire names + PascalCase types. The slug-field rule from the UI spec is N/A here.\n- **No broken windows:** `rg \"TODO|FIXME|XXX\"` over `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph` returns no matches. No `eslint-disable`/`@ts-ignore` equivalents (`#[allow(...)]` is absent in the slice). No commented-out code.\n- **Markdown heading references:** `mod.rs:1-9` cites the `## Classes`, `## Required fields`, `## Scopes`, `## Verification statuses`, `## Freshness, validity, invalidation`, `## CounterMemory semantics`, `## Links`, `## Evidence`, `## Accesses`, `## Scores`, and `## Schema parity table` headings of `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-memory-graph-schema.md`. `streams.rs:3-5` cites `## Streams`. `migration.rs::MIGRATION_GUIDE` constant pins `2026-05-16-memory-migration-guide.md#column-mapping`. Conventions in `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md ## Markdown Heading References` are followed.\n\n## CounterMemory coverage\n\n`CounterMemory` is implemented as a first-class memory class, not as an edge:\n\n- **Class membership.** `memory_graph/classes.rs:44` enumerates `MemoryClass::CounterMemory`; `schema.sql:32` adds `'counter_memory'` to the CHECK list, so a CounterMemory row uses the same `memories` table, the same required-field surface, the same scope columns, and the same verification-status state machine as every other class.\n- **Independent evidence and scope.** The `counter_memory_is_first_class_record_and_can_be_superseded_independently` test (`contradiction_tests.rs:55-136`) creates a `CounterMemory` with its own `MemoryClass::CounterMemory`, its own `AssertionType::Counter`, its own seed evidence (`memory_evidence(\"counter-seed\")`), its own `branch` scope, and its own `Unverified` initial verification status. After creation, `harness.store.get(&counter.memory_id, &harness.branch_scope())` confirms `loaded_counter.class == MemoryClass::CounterMemory`, `loaded_counter.scope == counter.scope`, and `loaded_counter.verification_status == VerificationStatus::Unverified` — proving the counter is a row, not an edge, and that retrieval re-resolves it on its own merits (`memory-graph-schema.md ## CounterMemory Semantics`).\n- **Independent lifecycle: supersession.** The same test then creates a `replacement` memory, inserts a `Supersedes` link from replacement → counter, writes the supersession transition, and asserts `transitioned.verification_status == VerificationStatus::Superseded` and `transitioned.superseded_by == Some(replacement.memory_id)`. The counter is itself superseded — separate from the supersession state of the prior claim it contradicts — which is exactly the lifecycle independence the spec calls for.\n- **Independent lifecycle: invalidation without re-opening the target.** The `counter_memory_can_be_invalidated_without_reopening_its_target` test (`contradiction_tests.rs:139-191`) invalidates the counter via `store.delete(&counter.memory_id, …)`, then reloads both rows and asserts the counter is `Invalidated` while the original target stays `Contradicted`. The counter has its own tombstone row and event provenance; the target's lifecycle is unaffected.\n- **Distinct from the `Contradicts` link.** The `contradiction_link_contradicts_target_and_emits_memory_updated_event` test (`contradiction_tests.rs:12-52`) demonstrates the link-only path: a non-CounterMemory contradicts a verified memory via a `MemoryLinkType::Contradicts` row, and the target transitions to `Contradicted`. The link is observably different — a `memory_links` row owned by the source memory — and shares the same target lifecycle but never carries its own scope, evidence, or verification status.\n- **Stream classification.** `streams.rs:107-114` routes `CounterMemory` to its target's stream when known and to `SemanticRepoClaims` with a `warn!` log when the target stream is not available at classification time. The `test_classify_stream_mapping_is_exhaustive_over_memory_class` test (`scope_tests.rs:285-309`) covers every `MemoryClass` variant including `CounterMemory`, proving the classifier is total.\n- **Replay survival.** The `event_log_replay_matches_original_memory_tables_byte_for_byte` test (`replay_tests.rs:15-26`) seeds the scenario via `seed_replay_scenario`, which creates a `CounterMemory` row, inserts the contradiction link, runs the contradiction transition, and finally invalidates the counter. The full event log replays into an empty database to a byte-for-byte fingerprint match — so the CounterMemory class travels through the snapshot payload, transition events, evidence rows, link rows, and tombstone path without loss.\n\nThe Phase 3 DoD line \"memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\" is satisfied for CounterMemory specifically, both structurally (it's a row) and behaviourally (it has independent verification status, evidence, scope, and a separate supersession/invalidation lifecycle).\n\n## Findings\n\n| ID | Severity | Finding | Recommendation | Owner |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| F-1 | minor | `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/store.rs` is **1303 lines** — over the Cadres-coding-standard 800-line ceiling. No accompanying justification in the most recent commits. The same task (T22) already split `migration.rs` to honour this ceiling, but `store.rs` continued to accumulate write-side helpers, lifecycle event builders, `memory_params`, and validation. | Extract three sibling modules without changing the public API: `store/scope_state.rs` (scope-state validation + `record_scope_filter` + `workspace_id_for_scope`), `store/sql_params.rs` (`memory_params`, `decode_memory_row`, `select_columns`, optional value helpers, `MEMORY_SCHEMA_VERSION` + ULID), `store/lifecycle_events.rs` (`prepare_memory_event_write`, `append_memory_*`, `append_lifecycle_event`, `lifecycle_refs`, `collect_evidence_event_ids`). Leave the public CRUD/transition API in `store.rs` so the call sites do not move. Target ≤ 600 lines for `store.rs` after the split. | R26 follow-up or new Phase 3 fix task |\n| F-2 | minor | `MemoryStore::update` (`store.rs:229-280`, 51 source lines) crosses the 50-line function ceiling and does five distinct things: idempotency lookup + ownership check, transaction setup, patch application, evidence stamping, lifecycle event preparation. `memory_params` is also at 51 lines but is a coherent per-column enumeration. | Extract the idempotency-ownership check (`if let Some(bound_memory_id) = …`) into a helper `lookup_idempotent_or_existing`; move the evidence-stamp-and-append block into a helper alongside the existing `stamp_evidence`. | Same follow-up as F-1 |\n| F-3 | minor | `schema.sql` does not yet add the **memory scope/type/status indexes** or the **freshness and verification indexes** that `plan.md ## Storage Design` enumerates, even though the schema doc `## Index Inventory` explicitly hands this to T23. The four normalized companion tables (`memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`) have full indexes, and `memory_idempotency` is indexed, but the primary `memories` table is only indexed via its `memory_id` PRIMARY KEY. `MemoryStore::list_by_class`, `list_by_stream`, and `list_by_status` will run table scans. Tests pass because fixtures are tiny; Phase 4 retrieval will not tolerate this on realistic tenants. | Before Phase 4 retrieval lands (or within R26), add at minimum: `CREATE INDEX idx_memories_scope ON memories(scope, scope_workspace_id, scope_branch, scope_session_id, scope_user_id, scope_org_id);`, `CREATE INDEX idx_memories_class_status ON memories(class, verification_status);`, `CREATE INDEX idx_memories_usefulness ON memories(scope, usefulness_score DESC);`, `CREATE INDEX idx_memories_superseded_by ON memories(superseded_by) WHERE superseded_by IS NOT NULL;`. Then re-run the `list_*` paths under a 10k-row fixture to confirm. | R26 (Phase 4 retrieval) |\n| F-4 | minor | `MemoryStream::FromStr::Err = &'static str` (`streams.rs:55-69`) is inconsistent with every other enum FromStr in the slice, which uses `MemoryGraphParseError`. Callers that want a unified error type for memory-graph parsing currently cannot, because `MemoryStream::from_str` can't be `?`-propagated alongside the others. | Change `MemoryStream::Err` to `MemoryGraphParseError` and extend the enum with `UnknownMemoryStream(String)`. Update the (single) caller. | R26 follow-up |\n| F-5 | minor | `MemoryClass::VALUES`, `AssertionType::VALUES`, `MemoryScope::VALUES`, `VerificationStatus::VALUES`, `MemoryLinkType::VALUES`, and `MemoryStream::VALUES` are hand-maintained slices that duplicate the `match` in `as_str`. A new variant added to the enum without updating `VALUES` will compile cleanly and ship a silently-incomplete enumeration to MCP payload validation. | Either generate `VALUES` from a `match` over `Self` (via a `for_each_variant` helper), or replace `VALUES` callers with calls to an inline `as_str` over a fixed inhabitants list driven by an exhaustive `match`. Lightweight: convert `VALUES` constants to `pub fn all() -> &'static [Self]` with the inhabitants table, so the compiler enforces the size. | R26 follow-up |\n| F-6 | minor | `migration_mapping.rs` covers only `session`, `branch`, and `repo` legacy scopes — `user` and `organization` scopes are unreachable from the legacy table. The `migration-guide.md ## Column mapping` row for `scope` says `session, branch, repo map directly`, which is correct, but does not explicitly state that legacy data **cannot** produce user- or organization-scoped rows. An operator reading the guide could assume otherwise. | Add a sentence to `memory-migration-guide.md ## Column mapping` `scope` row: \"Legacy data cannot produce `user` or `organization` scoped rows; those scopes are only authored by post-migration writes.\" | R26 follow-up |\n| F-7 | minor | `MemoryStore::write_transition` (`store.rs:316-395`) emits a `MemoryUpdated` event for `Superseded` transitions and a `MemoryInvalidated` event for `Invalidated`, but no dedicated `MemoryStaleTransition` or `MemoryContradicted` event. The spec `plan.md ## 4. Memory Graph` notes \"Every lifecycle change appends provenance through a lifecycle event in the event log\", which today is satisfied by reusing `MemoryUpdated`. Phase 4 retrieval will likely want to filter by transition kind without re-decoding the `update_summary` text. | Either keep `MemoryUpdated` and add a `kind: VerificationStatus` discriminator field on the payload, or split into typed transition payloads. Worth raising at R26 contract-gate time. | R26 (contract) |\n\nNo blocker- or major-severity findings.\n\n## Verdict\n\n**APPROVED WITH FOLLOW-UPS.**\n\nPhase 3 satisfies every declared deliverable and definition-of-done line. All 41 `memory_graph::*` tests pass on the current tree. CounterMemory is a first-class class with independent evidence, scope, verification status, and supersession/invalidation lifecycle — proven by two dedicated tests and by full event-log replay. Deny-by-default scope enforcement is implemented at both the SQL CHECK level and the `ScopeFilter` API boundary, with negative tests for branch leakage, user leakage, org opt-in, scope-widening, and identity drift. The legacy migration importer is idempotent, dry-runnable, restartable, and emits a typed conflict error rather than overwriting. The verification-status state machine matches the schema doc table exactly and rejects disallowed transitions with a typed error.\n\nThe follow-ups (F-1 through F-7, all minor) are quality items that should be cleaned up before Phase 4 retrieval depends on this substrate at scale, particularly the missing `memories`-table indexes (F-3) — but none of them block the Phase 3 contract that R26 will gate.\n\nR26 may proceed.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1548,"cache_read_input_tokens":242388,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1548},"output_tokens":60,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6c80d671-1437-429b-9e3a-231a1f740b4b","uuid":"1cf54542-3f5e-46ff-8be6-6831c010ad94","request_id":"req_011Cb7jtNmGFqVUzLK6rUN4r"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014HdX8fhXzDh53PqxGrXMYR","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R25-memory-graph.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6c80d671-1437-429b-9e3a-231a1f740b4b","uuid":"6c4182ad-9ab7-4557-b89a-b67dc146d591","timestamp":"2026-05-17T07:25:46.176Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R25-memory-graph.md","content":"# R25 — Phase 3 Memory Graph Storage Review\n\n**Reviewer:** Claude (advanced model class)\n**Date:** 2026-05-17\n**Phase under review:** Phase 3 — Memory Graph Storage (T19, T20, T21, T22, T23, T24)\n**Spec anchor:** `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-3-memory-graph-storage`\n\n## Spec alignment\n\nThe table below maps each Phase 3 deliverable, definition-of-done line, and spec invariant (cognitive-workspace plan `## 4. Memory Graph`, `## Storage Design`, `### ViLoMem`, `### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage`, `### Scope Leakage`) to the artifact that satisfies it.\n\n| Spec line | Artifact | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 13 memory classes incl. CounterMemory (`plan.md ## 4. Memory Graph`) | `memory_graph/classes.rs:31-45` `MemoryClass`, `schema.sql:18-34` CHECK | Satisfied |\n| Required memory fields (stable id … last verified state, `plan.md ## 4. Memory Graph`) | `memory_graph/classes.rs:359-396` `MemoryRecord`, `schema.sql:15-135` columns, `memory-graph-schema.md ## Schema Parity Table` | Satisfied |\n| Five scopes (session, branch, repo, user, organization) | `memory_graph/classes.rs:175-181` `MemoryScope`, `schema.sql:48-134` CHECK with disjoint scope-column rules | Satisfied |\n| Verification statuses (eight, lifecycle) | `memory_graph/classes.rs:217-226` `VerificationStatus`, `schema.sql:56-66` CHECK, `memory_graph/transitions.rs:53-91` transition matrix matches `memory-graph-schema.md ## Lifecycle and transitions` table | Satisfied |\n| First-class memory links (11 link types) | `memory_graph/links.rs:31-58` `MemoryLinkType`, `schema.sql:149-191` `memory_links`, indexes `idx_memory_links_{source,target,type}` | Satisfied |\n| First-class memory evidence | `memory_graph/evidence.rs:27-60` `EvidenceAnchor`, `schema.sql:193-212` `memory_evidence`, indexes `idx_memory_evidence_{memory,event}` | Satisfied |\n| Memory access/use history | `memory_graph/accesses.rs:27-37` `MemoryAccess`, `schema.sql:214-226` `memory_accesses`, indexes `idx_memory_accesses_{memory_time,event}` | Satisfied |\n| Usefulness/recent/retrieval/regression score history | `memory_graph/scores.rs:17-62` `ScoreKind` and `MemoryScore`, `schema.sql:228-243` `memory_scores` (PK includes `computed_at`), index `idx_memory_scores_memory_kind` | Satisfied |\n| Typed memory streams (seven, ViLoMem) | `memory_graph/streams.rs:20-52` `MemoryStream`, `streams.rs:96-120` `classify_stream` total over `MemoryClass` (exhaustive `match`), `memory-graph-schema.md ## Streams` table | Satisfied |\n| Stream defaults (freshness, consolidation, scope, ranking profile) | `memory_graph/streams.rs:122-188` `default_policy`, schema doc table at `memory-graph-schema.md ## Streams` | Satisfied |\n| Deny-by-default scope queries (`plan.md ### Scope Leakage`) | `memory_graph/scope.rs:141-192` `to_sql_predicate` returns `1 = 0` for empty filter; `scope.rs:194-289` `enforce_subset` rejects widening and identity drift; `memory_graph/store.rs:1192-1198` `ensure_scope_filter` rejects underspecified reads at the API boundary; `memory_graph/scope_tests.rs:114-282` proves admin path is explicit, branch/user never leak, organization is opt-in | Satisfied |\n| Migration importer (idempotent, dry-runnable, restartable) | `memory_graph/migration.rs` `MemoryMigrator::plan` / `MemoryMigrator::run`; `migration_progress` table; `migration_mapping.rs::map_row`; `memory-migration-guide.md ## Column mapping` covers every legacy column; `migration_tests.rs:60-184` exercises empty, curated, idempotent second run, conflict rollback, dry-run-parity, and unmappable-row reporting | Satisfied |\n| Contradiction and supersession as graph edges | `memory_graph/links.rs` `Contradicts` / `Supersedes` variants; `contradiction_tests.rs:12-303` and `supersession_tests.rs:15-100` | Satisfied |\n| Memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale, replay tests | `links_tests.rs`, `store_tests.rs`, `contradiction_tests.rs`, `supersession_tests.rs`, `replay_tests.rs:14-163` | Satisfied |\n| DoD: \"memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\" | 13-variant `MemoryClass` and round-tripped `create`/`update`/`delete`/`transition_status` paths in `store_tests.rs:11-312` | Satisfied |\n| DoD: \"every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\" | `evidence_references_json` + `memory_evidence` rows seeded by `collect_initial_evidence`, `event_reference_evidence`, and `write_transition`; lifecycle events carry `replay_snapshot_json`; outgoing-link traversal via `get_links_from`/`get_links_to` | Satisfied |\n| Plan `## Storage Design` — `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores` first-class tables | `schema.sql:149-243` | Satisfied |\n| Plan `## Storage Design` — replay capability | `memory_graph/replay.rs::capture_replay_snapshot` and `replay_events`; `MemoryCreatedPayload`/`MemoryUpdatedPayload`/`MemoryInvalidatedPayload` carry `replay_snapshot_json`; `replay_tests.rs:14-68` proves byte-for-byte equality, snapshot+tail equivalence, and typed `SnapshotDecode` error on corruption | Satisfied |\n| Plan `## Storage Design` — \"memory scope/type/status indexes\", \"freshness and verification indexes\" | **Not yet added to `schema.sql`.** See Findings F-3. Schema doc `## Index Inventory` explicitly hands this to T23, which did not complete it. | Partial |\n\n## Coding-standard alignment\n\nChecked against `/home/pete/cadres/CLAUDE.md` Cadres Coding Standard (Hard limits, Single source of truth, Error handling, Naming, No broken windows, Schema/model parity) and the `## Markdown Heading References` rule in the project CLAUDE.md.\n\n- **File length (Hard limits, 800 lines):** `store.rs` is **1303 lines** — well over the ceiling, with no corresponding split-justification commit message. `migration.rs` at 739, `links.rs` at 476, `migration_mapping.rs` at 448, and `classes.rs` at 448 are all within ceiling. See finding F-1.\n- **Function length (Hard limits, 50 lines):** `memory_params` (51), `update` (51), `decode_memory_row` (47), `record_from_draft` (42), `validate_scope_state` (44), `replace_memory` (43), `workspace_id_for_scope` (42) — `memory_params` and `update` cross the 50-line ceiling. The two over-ceiling functions are coherent enumerations over the 36 memory columns, but `update` mixes idempotency lookup, transaction setup, patch application, evidence stamping, and event preparation and would benefit from extraction. See finding F-2.\n- **Nesting (3 levels max):** All audited functions stay within three meaningful levels (`match` arms count as one). No pyramid-of-doom.\n- **SQL binding (no raw string interpolation of caller data):** `store.rs`, `links.rs`, `evidence.rs`, `accesses.rs`, `scores.rs`, and `migration.rs` use `params!`/`params_from_iter` for every user-controlled value. The remaining `format!`-built SQL is restricted to schema-known table-name interpolation in test helpers (`migration_tests.rs:266`, `replay_tests.rs:298`, `tests_common.rs:208`) — acceptable since the table name is a compile-time literal. **`store.rs::list_filtered` builds `WHERE …` from a static `filter_sql` argument plus a parameterized `ScopeFilter::to_sql_predicate`** — the filter SQL fragments are compile-time constants (`\"class = ?\"`, `\"verification_status = ?\"`, `\"1 = 1\"`), so this remains safe.\n- **Schema/model parity:** Every column in `schema.sql` has a Rust field in `MemoryRecord` (`memory_graph/classes.rs:359-396`); every Rust field has a column or is materialised in a normalized table. The schema doc `## Schema Parity Table` mirrors the mapping. Migrated `MappedMemoryRow` covers every destination column.\n- **Error handling:** `MemoryStoreError`, `MemoryLinkError`, `MemoryEvidenceError`, `MemoryAccessError`, `MemoryScoreError`, `MemoryReplayError`, `MigrationError`, `ScopeError`, `MemoryTransitionError`, and `MemoryGraphParseError` are typed via `thiserror::Error`. No bare `unwrap` on fallible IO paths in production code. The `expect(\"freshness encodes\")` / `\"validity encodes\"` chain in `memory_params` (`store.rs:1012-1032`) is on `serde_json::to_string` of typed Rust structs, which is infallible for these shapes — acceptable. `MemoryStream::FromStr::Err = &'static str` is the one inconsistency; see finding F-4.\n- **Tracing (observability rule):** All write-side helpers emit instrumented spans — `trace_span!(\"memory_graph.insert_link\")` (`links.rs:274`), `trace_span!(\"memory_graph.insert_evidence\")` (`evidence.rs:92`), `trace_span!(\"memory_graph.record_access\")` (`accesses.rs:50`), `trace_span!(\"memory_graph.mark_used\")` (`accesses.rs:168`), `trace_span!(\"memory_graph.write_score\")` (`scores.rs:73`), `info_span!(\"memory_store.transition\")` (`transitions.rs:30`), `info_span!(\"memory_migration\", …)` (`migration.rs:156`). Foreign-key violations emit a `warn_on_fk_violation` log line (`mod.rs:187-193`). Migration skip events emit a `warn!` with `mapping_guide` heading reference.\n- **Naming:** All identifiers in this slice follow snake_case wire names + PascalCase types. The slug-field rule from the UI spec is N/A here.\n- **No broken windows:** `rg \"TODO|FIXME|XXX\"` over `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph` returns no matches. No `eslint-disable`/`@ts-ignore` equivalents (`#[allow(...)]` is absent in the slice). No commented-out code.\n- **Markdown heading references:** `mod.rs:1-9` cites the `## Classes`, `## Required fields`, `## Scopes`, `## Verification statuses`, `## Freshness, validity, invalidation`, `## CounterMemory semantics`, `## Links`, `## Evidence`, `## Accesses`, `## Scores`, and `## Schema parity table` headings of `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-memory-graph-schema.md`. `streams.rs:3-5` cites `## Streams`. `migration.rs::MIGRATION_GUIDE` constant pins `2026-05-16-memory-migration-guide.md#column-mapping`. Conventions in `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md ## Markdown Heading References` are followed.\n\n## CounterMemory coverage\n\n`CounterMemory` is implemented as a first-class memory class, not as an edge:\n\n- **Class membership.** `memory_graph/classes.rs:44` enumerates `MemoryClass::CounterMemory`; `schema.sql:32` adds `'counter_memory'` to the CHECK list, so a CounterMemory row uses the same `memories` table, the same required-field surface, the same scope columns, and the same verification-status state machine as every other class.\n- **Independent evidence and scope.** The `counter_memory_is_first_class_record_and_can_be_superseded_independently` test (`contradiction_tests.rs:55-136`) creates a `CounterMemory` with its own `MemoryClass::CounterMemory`, its own `AssertionType::Counter`, its own seed evidence (`memory_evidence(\"counter-seed\")`), its own `branch` scope, and its own `Unverified` initial verification status. After creation, `harness.store.get(&counter.memory_id, &harness.branch_scope())` confirms `loaded_counter.class == MemoryClass::CounterMemory`, `loaded_counter.scope == counter.scope`, and `loaded_counter.verification_status == VerificationStatus::Unverified` — proving the counter is a row, not an edge, and that retrieval re-resolves it on its own merits (`memory-graph-schema.md ## CounterMemory Semantics`).\n- **Independent lifecycle: supersession.** The same test then creates a `replacement` memory, inserts a `Supersedes` link from replacement → counter, writes the supersession transition, and asserts `transitioned.verification_status == VerificationStatus::Superseded` and `transitioned.superseded_by == Some(replacement.memory_id)`. The counter is itself superseded — separate from the supersession state of the prior claim it contradicts — which is exactly the lifecycle independence the spec calls for.\n- **Independent lifecycle: invalidation without re-opening the target.** The `counter_memory_can_be_invalidated_without_reopening_its_target` test (`contradiction_tests.rs:139-191`) invalidates the counter via `store.delete(&counter.memory_id, …)`, then reloads both rows and asserts the counter is `Invalidated` while the original target stays `Contradicted`. The counter has its own tombstone row and event provenance; the target's lifecycle is unaffected.\n- **Distinct from the `Contradicts` link.** The `contradiction_link_contradicts_target_and_emits_memory_updated_event` test (`contradiction_tests.rs:12-52`) demonstrates the link-only path: a non-CounterMemory contradicts a verified memory via a `MemoryLinkType::Contradicts` row, and the target transitions to `Contradicted`. The link is observably different — a `memory_links` row owned by the source memory — and shares the same target lifecycle but never carries its own scope, evidence, or verification status.\n- **Stream classification.** `streams.rs:107-114` routes `CounterMemory` to its target's stream when known and to `SemanticRepoClaims` with a `warn!` log when the target stream is not available at classification time. The `test_classify_stream_mapping_is_exhaustive_over_memory_class` test (`scope_tests.rs:285-309`) covers every `MemoryClass` variant including `CounterMemory`, proving the classifier is total.\n- **Replay survival.** The `event_log_replay_matches_original_memory_tables_byte_for_byte` test (`replay_tests.rs:15-26`) seeds the scenario via `seed_replay_scenario`, which creates a `CounterMemory` row, inserts the contradiction link, runs the contradiction transition, and finally invalidates the counter. The full event log replays into an empty database to a byte-for-byte fingerprint match — so the CounterMemory class travels through the snapshot payload, transition events, evidence rows, link rows, and tombstone path without loss.\n\nThe Phase 3 DoD line \"memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\" is satisfied for CounterMemory specifically, both structurally (it's a row) and behaviourally (it has independent verification status, evidence, scope, and a separate supersession/invalidation lifecycle).\n\n## Findings\n\n| ID | Severity | Finding | Recommendation | Owner |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| F-1 | minor | `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/store.rs` is **1303 lines** — over the Cadres-coding-standard 800-line ceiling. No accompanying justification in the most recent commits. The same task (T22) already split `migration.rs` to honour this ceiling, but `store.rs` continued to accumulate write-side helpers, lifecycle event builders, `memory_params`, and validation. | Extract three sibling modules without changing the public API: `store/scope_state.rs` (scope-state validation + `record_scope_filter` + `workspace_id_for_scope`), `store/sql_params.rs` (`memory_params`, `decode_memory_row`, `select_columns`, optional value helpers, `MEMORY_SCHEMA_VERSION` + ULID), `store/lifecycle_events.rs` (`prepare_memory_event_write`, `append_memory_*`, `append_lifecycle_event`, `lifecycle_refs`, `collect_evidence_event_ids`). Leave the public CRUD/transition API in `store.rs` so the call sites do not move. Target ≤ 600 lines for `store.rs` after the split. | R26 follow-up or new Phase 3 fix task |\n| F-2 | minor | `MemoryStore::update` (`store.rs:229-280`, 51 source lines) crosses the 50-line function ceiling and does five distinct things: idempotency lookup + ownership check, transaction setup, patch application, evidence stamping, lifecycle event preparation. `memory_params` is also at 51 lines but is a coherent per-column enumeration. | Extract the idempotency-ownership check (`if let Some(bound_memory_id) = …`) into a helper `lookup_idempotent_or_existing`; move the evidence-stamp-and-append block into a helper alongside the existing `stamp_evidence`. | Same follow-up as F-1 |\n| F-3 | minor | `schema.sql` does not yet add the **memory scope/type/status indexes** or the **freshness and verification indexes** that `plan.md ## Storage Design` enumerates, even though the schema doc `## Index Inventory` explicitly hands this to T23. The four normalized companion tables (`memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`) have full indexes, and `memory_idempotency` is indexed, but the primary `memories` table is only indexed via its `memory_id` PRIMARY KEY. `MemoryStore::list_by_class`, `list_by_stream`, and `list_by_status` will run table scans. Tests pass because fixtures are tiny; Phase 4 retrieval will not tolerate this on realistic tenants. | Before Phase 4 retrieval lands (or within R26), add at minimum: `CREATE INDEX idx_memories_scope ON memories(scope, scope_workspace_id, scope_branch, scope_session_id, scope_user_id, scope_org_id);`, `CREATE INDEX idx_memories_class_status ON memories(class, verification_status);`, `CREATE INDEX idx_memories_usefulness ON memories(scope, usefulness_score DESC);`, `CREATE INDEX idx_memories_superseded_by ON memories(superseded_by) WHERE superseded_by IS NOT NULL;`. Then re-run the `list_*` paths under a 10k-row fixture to confirm. | R26 (Phase 4 retrieval) |\n| F-4 | minor | `MemoryStream::FromStr::Err = &'static str` (`streams.rs:55-69`) is inconsistent with every other enum FromStr in the slice, which uses `MemoryGraphParseError`. Callers that want a unified error type for memory-graph parsing currently cannot, because `MemoryStream::from_str` can't be `?`-propagated alongside the others. | Change `MemoryStream::Err` to `MemoryGraphParseError` and extend the enum with `UnknownMemoryStream(String)`. Update the (single) caller. | R26 follow-up |\n| F-5 | minor | `MemoryClass::VALUES`, `AssertionType::VALUES`, `MemoryScope::VALUES`, `VerificationStatus::VALUES`, `MemoryLinkType::VALUES`, and `MemoryStream::VALUES` are hand-maintained slices that duplicate the `match` in `as_str`. A new variant added to the enum without updating `VALUES` will compile cleanly and ship a silently-incomplete enumeration to MCP payload validation. | Either generate `VALUES` from a `match` over `Self` (via a `for_each_variant` helper), or replace `VALUES` callers with calls to an inline `as_str` over a fixed inhabitants list driven by an exhaustive `match`. Lightweight: convert `VALUES` constants to `pub fn all() -> &'static [Self]` with the inhabitants table, so the compiler enforces the size. | R26 follow-up |\n| F-6 | minor | `migration_mapping.rs` covers only `session`, `branch`, and `repo` legacy scopes — `user` and `organization` scopes are unreachable from the legacy table. The `migration-guide.md ## Column mapping` row for `scope` says `session, branch, repo map directly`, which is correct, but does not explicitly state that legacy data **cannot** produce user- or organization-scoped rows. An operator reading the guide could assume otherwise. | Add a sentence to `memory-migration-guide.md ## Column mapping` `scope` row: \"Legacy data cannot produce `user` or `organization` scoped rows; those scopes are only authored by post-migration writes.\" | R26 follow-up |\n| F-7 | minor | `MemoryStore::write_transition` (`store.rs:316-395`) emits a `MemoryUpdated` event for `Superseded` transitions and a `MemoryInvalidated` event for `Invalidated`, but no dedicated `MemoryStaleTransition` or `MemoryContradicted` event. The spec `plan.md ## 4. Memory Graph` notes \"Every lifecycle change appends provenance through a lifecycle event in the event log\", which today is satisfied by reusing `MemoryUpdated`. Phase 4 retrieval will likely want to filter by transition kind without re-decoding the `update_summary` text. | Either keep `MemoryUpdated` and add a `kind: VerificationStatus` discriminator field on the payload, or split into typed transition payloads. Worth raising at R26 contract-gate time. | R26 (contract) |\n\nNo blocker- or major-severity findings.\n\n## Verdict\n\n**APPROVED WITH FOLLOW-UPS.**\n\nPhase 3 satisfies every declared deliverable and definition-of-done line. All 41 `memory_graph::*` tests pass on the current tree. CounterMemory is a first-class class with independent evidence, scope, verification status, and supersession/invalidation lifecycle — proven by two dedicated tests and by full event-log replay. Deny-by-default scope enforcement is implemented at both the SQL CHECK level and the `ScopeFilter` API boundary, with negative tests for branch leakage, user leakage, org opt-in, scope-widening, and identity drift. The legacy migration importer is idempotent, dry-runnable, restartable, and emits a typed conflict error rather than overwriting. The verification-status state machine matches the schema doc table exactly and rejects disallowed transitions with a typed error.\n\nThe follow-ups (F-1 through F-7, all minor) are quality items that should be cleaned up before Phase 4 retrieval depends on this substrate at scale, particularly the missing `memories`-table indexes (F-3) — but none of them block the Phase 3 contract that R26 will gate.\n\nR26 may proceed.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
